// rbac domain -- capability catalog writes.
//
// Authz tier: PRIVILEGED catalog write (not per-user-owned). Writing the
// RBAC grant catalog is a cluster-administration action; the capability
// rows are global (_system slot) reference data exactly like the
// v1:agents:agentRole / v1:skills:skill catalogs. In E1.1 the only writer
// is the SeedMaterializer (running as the `system:seedMaterializer`
// actor), which walks the `seed capability ...` declarations under
// dsl/rbac/*.memql and stamps each body's `id` (the seed name) into
// `capabilityId`. The RANK-BOUNDED custom-role authoring path (a creator
// may only mint grants at or below their own rank) lands in E1.4
// (memql#2074) as logic that wraps this mutation; the base-role grants
// this mutation seeds are marked predefined=true and frozen at runtime by
// E1.2 (memql#2070). The args surface mirrors the v1:rbac:capability
// concept field-for-field, matching the materializer's
// mutationCreate<Concept> convention.

/// SERVER-ONLY (epic memql#5166). The client-reachable path is `roleCreate` in builtins.memql,
/// which applies the guards this mutation has none of: rank strictly below the creator, a rank
/// no rung already holds, a slug no role or alias already claims, and every grant one the
/// creator holds. A client reaching this mutation directly would write a role at any rank
/// holding anything, which is the whole of the escalation the builtins exist to refuse.
///
/// CALLER-SCOPING IS NOT THE FIX HERE, and that is the argument @serverOnly always owes. A
/// filter on actor.userId narrows WHICH ROWS a caller sees; the danger on this mutation is not
/// which role row you read but what rank and what grants you write into a new one. The role
/// catalog is deployment-wide reference data with no owner field to scope by -- every principal
/// reads the whole ladder, by design, because a client that cannot see a rung cannot rank it --
/// so there is no per-row predicate that could express "you may not mint a role above yourself".
/// That rule is relational (creator's rank versus the new rank) and set-valued (the grants
/// against the creator's own), which is Go, which is `roleCreate`.
///
/// The SeedMaterializer still writes through it, because that path stamps internal origin
/// (component/memql/seed_materializer.go) -- which is what @serverOnly admits and a client
/// cannot forge.
///
/// Insert a v1:rbac:role catalog row -- a named, ranked capability bundle. Called by the
/// SeedMaterializer when it walks the base-role seed declarations under dsl/rbac/seeds.memql (the
/// materializer stamps the seed body's `id` into `roleId`). Also the write surface the E1.4
/// (memql#2072) rank-bounded custom-role authoring path layers on top of -- predefined defaults
/// false there so the role stays editable within the creator's rank bound, and the creator's own
/// rank caps the `rank` arg. Base-role seeds pass predefined=true to mark the row IMMUTABLE at
/// runtime: the validateRbacRoleImmutable Go guard (E1.2) rejects any non-system-actor write to a
/// predefined role, so a base role can never be redefined / re-ranked as data to escalate
/// privilege. Partition-agnostic: the engine stamps every v1:rbac:role insert into the _system slot
/// regardless of the envelope, exactly like createAgentRole / createCapability.
@serverOnly
mutate role createRole {
  args {
    roleId       string
    slug         string!
    name         string!
    rank         integer!
    description  string
    predefined   bool
    active       bool
    /// The account this role is scoped to (D10), or empty for a global role.
    /// Accepted here for the reason `aliases` is: the guarded authoring path
    /// (`roleCreate`) writes through this mutation, and a field the mutation
    /// does not accept is a field the write silently drops.
    accountId    string
    /// Other slugs that resolve to THIS rung. Accepted because the seed
    /// materializer writes base roles through this mutation, and a field the
    /// mutation does not accept is a field the seed silently drops -- which is
    /// exactly what happened to `aliases` on its first attempt: every seeded
    /// row landed with it null, the engine kept working through its compiled
    /// fallback, and MemQL OS could not rank `writer` or `reader` at all.
    aliases      []string
  }
  insert {
    accept { slug, name, rank, description, aliases, accountId }
    stamp {
      id: args.roleId ?? args.slug
      predefined: args.predefined ?? false
      active: args.active ?? true
    }
  }
}

/// SERVER-ONLY, for `createRole`'s reason (epic memql#5166): a grant written directly is a grant
/// nothing checked the writer held. The guarded path is `roleCreate` / `roleUpdate`, which write
/// the capability rows for a role in the same internal-origin write that writes the role.
///
/// Scoping to actor.userId would not help and could not be written: a capability row belongs to a
/// ROLE rather than to a person, carries no owner field, and is read by every principal on every
/// request -- the catalog is public reference data by declaration. The rule that has to hold is
/// "you may not grant what you do not hold", which compares the writer's resolved grant set
/// against the row being written; no per-row filter can say that.
///
/// Insert a v1:rbac:capability catalog row -- one (verb x resourceType) grant for a role. Called by
/// the SeedMaterializer when it walks the capability seed declarations under dsl/rbac/*.memql (the
/// materializer stamps the seed body's `id` into `capabilityId`). Also the write surface the E1.4
/// (memql#2074) rank-bounded custom-role authoring path layers on top of -- predefined defaults
/// false there so the grant stays editable within the creator's rank bound; base-role seeds pass
/// predefined=true to mark the row immutable at runtime (enforced by E1.2). Partition-agnostic: the
/// engine stamps every v1:rbac:capability insert into the _system slot regardless of the envelope,
/// exactly like createAgentRole / createSkill.
@serverOnly
mutate capability createCapability {
  args {
    capabilityId  string
    roleSlug      string!
    verb          enum("read", "create", "update", "delete", "execute")!
    resourceType  string!
    effect        enum("allow", "deny")
    description   string
    predefined    bool
    active        bool
  }
  insert {
    accept { roleSlug, verb, resourceType, description }
    stamp {
      id: args.capabilityId
      effect: args.effect ?? "allow"
      predefined: args.predefined ?? false
      active: args.active ?? true
    }
  }
}

/// Write one v1:rbac:grant row -- a person's or a group's allow or deny over one (verb, resourceType)
/// -- as a NEW VERSION at the derived id (epic memql#5294). Re-granting the same subject the same
/// pair writes another version of the same logical row; it never mints a second one.
///
/// @serverOnly, and actor.userId scoping is worse than merely unavailable here. The obvious owner
/// field would be `subjectId`, the person the grant is FOR -- and an owned row admits its owner's
/// INSERTS, so scoping to actor.userId would hand every signed-in person a primitive for granting
/// themselves an app. The row is the deployment's record of a decision (the concept declares
/// @rowAuthz(clusterOwner, rankFloor="admin") and carries no owner field), and the rule that has to
/// hold before it is written -- the writer holds `update` on `principal`, holds the capability
/// being granted, outranks the subject, and is not the subject -- is relational, which is Go, which
/// is the guarded `grantSet` path. A client reaching this directly would skip all four.
@serverOnly
mutate grant writeGrant {
  args {
    /// The derived row id: auth.GrantRowID(subjectKind, subjectId, verb, resourceType).
    grantId       string!
    subjectKind   enum("user", "group")!
    subjectId     string!
    verb          enum("read", "create", "update", "delete", "execute")!
    resourceType  string!
    effect        enum("allow", "deny")!
    grantedBy     string
  }
  insert {
    accept { subjectKind, subjectId, verb, resourceType, effect }
    stamp {
      id:        args.grantId
      grantedBy: args.grantedBy ?? ""
      active:    true
    }
  }
}

/// Revoke one v1:rbac:grant by writing `active: false` at its derived id -- the read-merge keeps
/// every other field, so the decision and its reversal are both history (epic memql#5294).
///
/// @serverOnly for writeGrant's reason: a person may not revoke a deny that bars them, and nothing
/// about the row says whose it is -- the concept carries no owner field, so there is no
/// actor.userId conjunct that could distinguish the writer who may from the subject who may not.
/// The guarded path is `grantRevoke`, which checks the writer's authority in Go and stamps
/// internal origin.
@serverOnly
mutate grant deactivateGrant {
  args {
    grantId  string!
  }
  update {
    id:     args.grantId
    active: false
  }
}
